(1155970, 136772, 31408, '<td valign="top">Thanks for everyone who "got" the image. This is really a one of a kind image and was quite difficult to shoot and light properly. I used a reflector to help "push" the light inside the egg area.<br/> <br/>I agree with all of the critics about the eye focus. The problem was that the head is still inside the egg and still coved by thick gelatinous protein (just like chicken eggs whites). So getting that area into sharp focus was something I didnt accomplish with this shot. But I did get the nose and strands in very clear focus, so the image worked on that level. As for the crop, it''s tight to show the extream detail and the position is "true life".<br/> <br/>There were several comments made out of patent ignorance of the subject matter. Those were interesting to read, even if a bit melodramatic. Any reaction is good feedback, and I already knew the icky factor would be there for some.<br/> <br/>Im not thrilled coming in at 372 out of 531 with this image. But then again, I could have played it safe with another image if the score was that important I guess.<br/> <br/>My congrats to the few that identified this as a Retic!</td>', 'Thanks for everyone who "got" the image. This is really a one of a kind image and was quite difficult to shoot and light properly. I used a reflector to help "push" the light inside the egg area. I agree with all of the critics about the eye focus. The problem was that the head is still inside the egg and still coved by thick gelatinous protein (just like chicken eggs whites). So getting that area into sharp focus was something I didnt accomplish with this shot. But I did get the nose and strands in very clear focus, so the image worked on that level. As for the crop, it''s tight to show the extream detail and the position is "true life". There were several comments made out of patent ignorance of the subject matter. Those were interesting to read, even if a bit melodramatic. Any reaction is good feedback, and I already knew the icky factor would be there for some. Im not thrilled coming in at 372 out of 531 with this image. But then again, I could have played it safe with another image if the score was that important I guess. My congrats to the few that identified this as a Retic!', '2005-02-07 21:05:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(1156014, 133723, 14986, '<td valign="top">I really like the tones here and the contrasts.  I don''t think it''s too dark...but that''s how I like my shots too.   Beautiful scene, captured very well.</td>', 'I really like the tones here and the contrasts.  I don''t think it''s too dark...but that''s how I like my shots too.   Beautiful scene, captured very well.', '2005-02-07 21:20:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(1157641, 136414, 12611, '<td valign="top">Thuis was great- the contrast was fantastic- don''t listen to anyone else- this is top notch. If there is any room to improve it would be in the area of composition. But thats a hell of a poster shot.</td>', 'Thuis was great- the contrast was fantastic- don''t listen to anyone else- this is top notch. If there is any room to improve it would be in the area of composition. But thats a hell of a poster shot.', '2005-02-08 15:46:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(1157658, 135927, 12611, '<td valign="top">This is great. Absolutely great b and w nature landscape. I love the texture of the puffy clouds back against the jagged snow on the mountain tips. nice nice work. This is top five no matter what anybody says.</td>', 'This is great. Absolutely great b and w nature landscape. I love the texture of the puffy clouds back against the jagged snow on the mountain tips. nice nice work. This is top five no matter what anybody says.', '2005-02-08 15:53:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(1161445, 135633, 19010, '<td valign="top">for sale!! //tristalisk.dpcprints.com</td>', 'for sale!! //tristalisk.dpcprints.com', '2005-02-10 08:38:23.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(1167971, 136300, 26487, '<td valign="top">This picture makes me feel so good. So much aesthetic. Thank you for your art that makes my heart flies.</td>', 'This picture makes me feel so good. So much aesthetic. Thank you for your art that makes my heart flies.', '2005-02-14 06:11:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(1168345, 134128, 33560, '<td valign="top">The sky is drammatic.  The hill is ordinary.  And the photo does not bear up under scrutiny; it becomes two photos.  Perhaps it would be better if most of the land were cropped a bit below the ridge.  I would darken the foreground which is in shadow and brighten/saturate the hill in a kind of mirrored effect to what you did the sky.  Then we would see the drama of the sunlit hill more as you and your friend did.  Just an idea.</td>', 'The sky is drammatic.  The hill is ordinary.  And the photo does not bear up under scrutiny; it becomes two photos.  Perhaps it would be better if most of the land were cropped a bit below the ridge.  I would darken the foreground which is in shadow and brighten/saturate the hill in a kind of mirrored effect to what you did the sky.  Then we would see the drama of the sunlit hill more as you and your friend did.  Just an idea.', '2005-02-14 10:03:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(1168410, 132622, 33560, '<td valign="top">When does it cross from being a photo to being a photo-derived work of art?  It''s a subjective question but I think this one has crossed the line.  At first glance the colors are interesting, drammatic, striking. But seconds later we stop thinking about the picture and start thinking about the steps taken to make it.  Then we start wondering whether the sky really would have been better without that great blob of red.  Or with less of that inky blue.  And then, finally, the chartreuse of the grass begins to grate.  And we begin to wonder what it was we saw in the photo in that first second.  That was my experience, anyway.  For the first second or so, it was a real kick.</td>', 'When does it cross from being a photo to being a photo-derived work of art?  It''s a subjective question but I think this one has crossed the line.  At first glance the colors are interesting, drammatic, striking. But seconds later we stop thinking about the picture and start thinking about the steps taken to make it.  Then we start wondering whether the sky really would have been better without that great blob of red.  Or with less of that inky blue.  And then, finally, the chartreuse of the grass begins to grate.  And we begin to wonder what it was we saw in the photo in that first second.  That was my experience, anyway.  For the first second or so, it was a real kick.', '2005-02-14 10:31:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(1168461, 132264, 33560, '<td valign="top">The subject is highly original.  The shapes are very interesting.  The lighting is ideal.  And the textures of the wood and grass clearly make this rustic setting remarkable.   It''s a really strong shot.<br/> <br/>While having the sea in the background adds a sense of place to the photo, I found the additional horizontal lines it offered both cluttered and confused by their close proximity to the tops of the houses.  It''s a quibble; and I''m not sure there could have been a way to fix this without ruining the shot.</td>', 'The subject is highly original.  The shapes are very interesting.  The lighting is ideal.  And the textures of the wood and grass clearly make this rustic setting remarkable.   It''s a really strong shot. While having the sea in the background adds a sense of place to the photo, I found the additional horizontal lines it offered both cluttered and confused by their close proximity to the tops of the houses.  It''s a quibble; and I''m not sure there could have been a way to fix this without ruining the shot.', '2005-02-14 10:47:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(1169432, 133848, 20774, '<td valign="top">This is another lovely shot you have here... very nice post process and ni love the compistion, the seagulls, the light house and the boat are just really nice on this photo. great shot overall i gave it an 8... very nice</td>', 'This is another lovely shot you have here... very nice post process and ni love the compistion, the seagulls, the light house and the boat are just really nice on this photo. great shot overall i gave it an 8... very nice', '2005-02-14 18:57:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(1169619, 133848, 12311, '<td valign="top">Jen, this is a beautiful shot of the Sodus Bay lighthouse.  I live just outside of Buffalo and spent a night there last summer.  While I didn''t vote on your photo, I would have given it an 8 or 9.  While I was at Sodus bay, I took this photo:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=147167" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/12311/thumb/147167.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/12311/thumb/147167.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/12311/thumb/147167.jpg"/></a> <br/>Yours just seems much "crisper".   Keep up the great work!<br/></td>', 'Jen, this is a beautiful shot of the Sodus Bay lighthouse.  I live just outside of Buffalo and spent a night there last summer.  While I didn''t vote on your photo, I would have given it an 8 or 9.  While I was at Sodus bay, I took this photo: Yours just seems much "crisper".   Keep up the great work!', '2005-02-14 19:53:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(1169978, 133544, 33560, '<td valign="top">I found this to be a stunning shot.  And I thought it deserved better treatment by the judges.  Then I considered the proposition that the moon is too small to be a feature.  In the sky this is not so big a deal.  But in the water its proximity ot the rock draws power from the rock - which is otherwise a very powerful focal point of  the photo.  Then it occurred to me: now that the competition is over, why not try a version with the moon removed. The clone stamp in Photoshop would make this quick work.  And you may end up with an even more strikingly beautiful photo.  Just a thought  - tell me if you do.<br/></td>', 'I found this to be a stunning shot.  And I thought it deserved better treatment by the judges.  Then I considered the proposition that the moon is too small to be a feature.  In the sky this is not so big a deal.  But in the water its proximity ot the rock draws power from the rock - which is otherwise a very powerful focal point of  the photo.  Then it occurred to me: now that the competition is over, why not try a version with the moon removed. The clone stamp in Photoshop would make this quick work.  And you may end up with an even more strikingly beautiful photo.  Just a thought  - tell me if you do.', '2005-02-14 22:23:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(1183049, 134026, 14986, '<td valign="top">Nice scoring shot and I can see why.  I gave you a 7 during the challenge for this one.  Some folks might think those colors are oversaturated, but I happen to really like it that way.  It would make a beautiful print, as it feels very artsy to me.  The reflections are awesome.  The only thing I would change, and you really have little control over, is the lighting.  It appears to be just a little flat.  Everything else is so nice, that it''s not something I''d notice right away, however.  It might look a little livelier with some dodging of the upper trees just a bit.    Hope this is helpful!</td>', 'Nice scoring shot and I can see why.  I gave you a 7 during the challenge for this one.  Some folks might think those colors are oversaturated, but I happen to really like it that way.  It would make a beautiful print, as it feels very artsy to me.  The reflections are awesome.  The only thing I would change, and you really have little control over, is the lighting.  It appears to be just a little flat.  Everything else is so nice, that it''s not something I''d notice right away, however.  It might look a little livelier with some dodging of the upper trees just a bit.    Hope this is helpful!', '2005-02-19 22:19:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(1183450, 136673, 33560, '<td valign="top">I''m not sure the title serves the photo well.  In my first look at the photo I was slightly puzzled at the half a face.  Then I realized that the choice was ideal and inspired.  The photo is not at all about this man.  It is about this girl''s relationship to him - what she feels about him.  He only exists in the photo to inform us of the object of her affection.  I think this is perfectly communicated by the way the subject is in crisp focus and is perfectly lit, while her subject of attention is the man.  The warmth of feeling here is powerful.  <br/> <br/>The photo has the emotional strength of a great candid and the technical strength of a posed or composed shot.  Excellent.</td>', 'I''m not sure the title serves the photo well.  In my first look at the photo I was slightly puzzled at the half a face.  Then I realized that the choice was ideal and inspired.  The photo is not at all about this man.  It is about this girl''s relationship to him - what she feels about him.  He only exists in the photo to inform us of the object of her affection.  I think this is perfectly communicated by the way the subject is in crisp focus and is perfectly lit, while her subject of attention is the man.  The warmth of feeling here is powerful.   The photo has the emotional strength of a great candid and the technical strength of a posed or composed shot.  Excellent.', '2005-02-20 07:32:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(1184411, 136424, 33942, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by dsa157:</b></div><hr/><i> assuming that this is not a faked image, it certainly has a wow factor to it. I can see some of the plane''s shadow on what I assume is the runway, but it seems like the wings should be casting shadows over the spectators on the beach. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>The shadow on the runway is from the wings. Look at the angle of the shadow on the side of the plane.</td>', 'Originally posted by dsa157: assuming that this is not a faked image, it certainly has a wow factor to it. I can see some of the plane''s shadow on what I assume is the runway, but it seems like the wings should be casting shadows over the spectators on the beach.   The shadow on the runway is from the wings. Look at the angle of the shadow on the side of the plane.', '2005-02-20 19:38:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(1188946, 136307, 31738, '<td valign="top">Wow, this photo is amazing, it''s so sensual and beautiful and the soft shadows look great. This really struck me and got my attention because there is such meaning behind it. Great work!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-02-25 12:14:02.</i></td>', 'Wow, this photo is amazing, it''s so sensual and beautiful and the soft shadows look great. This really struck me and got my attention because there is such meaning behind it. Great work!', '2005-02-22 18:44:13.000000', '2005-02-25 12:14:02.000000', 0),
(1190138, 136675, 33701, '<td valign="top">lovely &amp; nice.</td>', 'lovely & nice.', '2005-02-23 06:24:31.000000', NULL, 0),
